# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr "Background {#background}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Every IBM Quantum&reg; QPU reports a set of properties that describe how well each of its qubits is currently working: relaxation times ($T_1$), coherence times ($T_2$), readout errors, and one- and two-qubit gate errors. These numbers matter in practice. The transpiler uses them to decide which physical qubits your circuit should run on, error mitigation techniques rely on them, and you might use them to judge whether a device is working well enough for an experiment.\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat IBM Quantum&reg; QPU ay nag-uulat ng isang set ng mga property na naglalarawan kung gaano kaayos kasalukuyang gumagana ang bawat isa sa mga qubit nito: relaxation times ($T_1$), coherence times ($T_2$), readout errors, at one- at two-qubit gate errors. Ang mga numerong ito ay mahalaga sa praktika. Ginagamit ito ng transpiler upang magpasya kung anong physical qubit ang dapat patakbuhin ng iyong circuit, umaasa dito ang mga error mitigation technique, at maaari mo itong gamitin upang husgahan kung sapat na kaayos ang isang device para sa isang eksperimento.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The reported properties come from calibration procedures that typically run about once a day. Superconducting qubits, however, can drift on shorter timescales, and transient defects (so-called two-level systems) can temporarily degrade a qubit that looked excellent at calibration time. In addition, a job is often transpiled well before it actually executes, so decisions based on the reported properties can rest on stale information. The tutorial [Real-time benchmarking for qubit selection](/tutorials/real-time-benchmarking-for-qubit-selection) shows how much these properties can fluctuate from day to day, and builds a full characterization workflow by hand from individual [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/).\n"
msgstr "Ang mga iniulat na property ay nagmumula sa mga calibration procedure na karaniwang tumatakbo minsan sa isang araw. Ang mga superconducting qubit, gayunpaman, ay maaaring mag-drift sa mas maiikling timescale, at ang mga transient defect (tinatawag na two-level systems) ay maaaring pansamantalang mapababa ang kalidad ng isang qubit na tila napakahusay noong panahon ng calibration. Bukod dito, ang isang job ay madalas na na-transpile nang matagal bago ito talagang mag-execute, kaya ang mga desisyong nakabatay sa mga iniulat na property ay maaaring nakabase sa lumang impormasyon. Ipinapakita ng tutorial na [Real-time benchmarking for qubit selection](/tutorials/real-time-benchmarking-for-qubit-selection) kung gaano kalaki ang maaaring pag-iba-ibahan ng mga property na ito sa bawat araw, at bumubuo ng isang buong characterization workflow nang manu-mano mula sa mga indibidwal na [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial shows the streamlined version of that workflow, by using the `BackendCharacterization` utility from [Qiskit Device Benchmarking](https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-benchmarking), which packages the experiment construction, job submission, and curve fitting into a few calls. You spend roughly 70 seconds of QPU time and get back a backend object whose properties reflect the device *right now*, ready to be compared against the reported values or passed on to downstream tools.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng tutorial na ito ang streamlined na bersyon ng workflow na iyon, sa pamamagitan ng paggamit ng `BackendCharacterization` utility mula sa [Qiskit Device Benchmarking](https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-benchmarking), na pinagsasama-sama ang pagbuo ng eksperimento, pagsusumite ng job, at curve fitting sa ilang tawag lamang. Gumugugol ka ng humigit-kumulang 70 segundo ng oras ng QPU at makakakuha ka ng backend object na ang mga property ay sumasalamin sa device *ngayon mismo*, handa nang ihambing sa mga iniulat na halaga o ipasa sa mga downstream na tool.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The characterization experiments {#the-characterization-experiments}"
msgstr "The characterization experiments {#the-characterization-experiments}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The suite measures five properties, each with a standard experiment:\n"
msgstr "Sinusukat ng suite ang limang property, bawat isa ay may standard na eksperimento:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Readout error** (`readout`): Each qubit is prepared in $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ and immediately measured. The probability of reading out the wrong state gives the state preparation and measurement (SPAM) error.\n"
msgstr "**Readout error** (`readout`): Ang bawat qubit ay inihahanda sa $|0\\rangle$ o $|1\\rangle$ at agad na sinusukat. Ang probability ng pagbasa ng maling state ay nagbibigay ng state preparation and measurement (SPAM) error.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Single-qubit gate error** (`rb_1q`): Randomized benchmarking (RB) runs sequences of random single-qubit Clifford gates that ideally compose to the identity. The way the survival probability decays with sequence length yields the average error per gate, independent of SPAM errors.\n"
msgstr "**Single-qubit gate error** (`rb_1q`): Ang Randomized benchmarking (RB) ay nagpapatakbo ng mga sequence ng random na single-qubit Clifford gate na sa ideal ay bumubuo sa identity. Ang paraan ng pagbaba ng survival probability habang tumataas ang haba ng sequence ay nagbibigay ng average error kada gate, independiyente sa mga SPAM error.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Two-qubit gate error** (`rb_2q`): The same RB idea applied to full layers of non-overlapping two-qubit gates executed simultaneously, as in a [layer fidelity](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05933) experiment. Because many gates run at once, the resulting error rates include crosstalk effects and are closer to what a deep circuit actually experiences.\n"
msgstr "**Two-qubit gate error** (`rb_2q`): Ang parehong ideya ng RB na inilalapat sa buong layer ng non-overlapping two-qubit gate na sabay-sabay na isinasagawa, tulad ng sa isang eksperimentong [layer fidelity](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05933). Dahil maraming gate ang tumatakbo nang sabay-sabay, ang resultang error rate ay kasama ang mga epekto ng crosstalk at mas malapit sa aktwal na naeeksperyensya ng isang malalim na circuit.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$T_1$** (`t1`): Each qubit is excited and measured after increasing delays. The decay of the excited-state population gives the relaxation time.\n"
msgstr "**$T_1$** (`t1`): Ang bawat qubit ay pinasisigla at sinusukat pagkatapos ng lumalaking delay. Ang pagbaba ng excited-state population ay nagbibigay ng relaxation time.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$T_2$** (`t2`): A Hahn echo experiment puts each qubit in a superposition, applies an echo pulse halfway through a delay, and measures how quickly phase coherence is lost. By default a single echo is used; a multi-echo (CPMG-style) variant is demonstrated at the end of this tutorial.\n"
msgstr "**$T_2$** (`t2`): Ang isang Hahn echo experiment ay naglalagay sa bawat qubit sa isang superposition, naglalapat ng echo pulse sa kalagitnaan ng isang delay, at sinusukat kung gaano kabilis nawawala ang phase coherence. Bilang default, ginagamit ang iisang echo; ipinapakita ang isang multi-echo (CPMG-style) na variant sa dulo ng tutorial na ito.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "What refreshing does and does not do {#what-refreshing-does-and-does-not-do}"
msgstr "What refreshing does and does not do {#what-refreshing-does-and-does-not-do}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Keep three points in mind when using this workflow:\n"
msgstr "Tandaan ang tatlong punto kapag gumagamit ng workflow na ito:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The refreshed properties live only in the backend object returned to you. Nothing changes on the device itself or in the calibration data that IBM Quantum reports to other users.\n"
msgstr "Nabubuhay lamang ang mga na-refresh na property sa backend object na ibinalik sa iyo. Walang nagbabago sa mismong device o sa calibration data na iniulat ng IBM Quantum sa ibang gumagamit.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The measured values are a snapshot taken with a particular methodology. Some of them, notably the layered two-qubit errors and the parallel single-echo $T_2$ values, are measured differently from the reported calibration data, so part of any gap you observe is methodological rather than drift. This tutorial points out where that happens.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga sinukat na halaga ay isang snapshot na kinuha gamit ang isang partikular na methodology. Ang ilan sa mga ito, lalo na ang layered two-qubit error at ang parallel single-echo $T_2$ na halaga, ay sinusukat nang iba mula sa iniulat na calibration data, kaya bahagi ng anumang pagkakaiba na iyong maoobserbahan ay methodological sa halip na drift. Itinuturo ng tutorial na ito kung saan ito nangyayari.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The characterization itself costs QPU time (about 70 seconds for the full suite on a Heron r2 processor), which is the price of up-to-date information.\n"
msgstr "Ang characterization mismo ay nagkakahalaga ng oras ng QPU (humigit-kumulang 70 segundo para sa buong suite sa isang Heron r2 processor), na siyang presyo ng up-to-date na impormasyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow follows the four steps of a Qiskit pattern:\n"
msgstr "Sinusunod ng workflow ang apat na hakbang ng isang Qiskit pattern:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem.** Choose the device and the set of properties to measure.\n"
msgstr "**Hakbang 1: I-map ang mga classical input sa isang quantum na problema.** Piliin ang device at ang set ng mga property na susukatin.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution.** The utility builds the experiment circuits directly in the device's native gates and parallelizes them across the chip.\n"
msgstr "**Hakbang 2: I-optimize ang problema para sa pagpapatakbo sa quantum hardware.** Direktang binubuo ng utility ang mga experiment circuit sa native gates ng device at pinararalel ang mga ito sa buong chip.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives.** The experiments run as Sampler jobs inside a batch.\n"
msgstr "**Hakbang 3: Mag-execute gamit ang Qiskit primitives.** Tumatakbo ang mga eksperimento bilang Sampler job sa loob ng isang batch.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format.** Fit the measurement data into per-qubit error maps, refresh the backend, and compare the measured properties against the reported ones.\n"
msgstr "**Hakbang 4: I-post-process at ibalik ang resulta sa nais na classical format.** I-fit ang measurement data sa mga per-qubit error map, i-refresh ang backend, at ihambing ang mga sinukat na property laban sa mga iniulat.\n"

